## Proof

We seek all real $\alpha$ for which there exists a real $2005\times 2005$ matrix $A$ satisfying
$$5A^2+(6\alpha-2)A+(2\alpha^2-2\alpha+1)I=0.$$

**Step 1: Compute the discriminant of the associated quadratic.**

The scalar polynomial is $p(x)=5x^2+(6\alpha-2)x+(2\alpha^2-2\alpha+1)$. Its discriminant is
$$\Delta=(6\alpha-2)^2-4\cdot 5\cdot(2\alpha^2-2\alpha+1)$$
$$=36\alpha^2-24\alpha+4-40\alpha^2+40\alpha-20=-4\alpha^2+16\alpha-16=-4(\alpha-2)^2\le 0,$$
with equality if and only if $\alpha=2$.

**Step 2: Case $\alpha\neq 2$ — no solution exists.**

When $\alpha\neq 2$, $\Delta<0$, so $p(x)$ has two non-real complex conjugate roots $\lambda,\bar\lambda$. Since $p(A)=0$, the minimal polynomial of $A$ divides $p(x)$, so every eigenvalue of $A$ is either $\lambda$ or $\bar\lambda$.

For a **real** matrix, non-real eigenvalues occur in conjugate pairs with equal algebraic multiplicities. If $\lambda$ has multiplicity $m$ and $\bar\lambda$ has multiplicity $m$, the total degree of the characteristic polynomial is $2m$, which is **even**. But the characteristic polynomial has degree $2005$, which is **odd**. This is a contradiction.

Therefore no real $2005\times 2005$ matrix $A$ satisfies the equation when $\alpha\neq 2$.

**Step 3: Case $\alpha=2$ — a solution exists.**

When $\alpha=2$:
$$p(x)=5x^2+10x+5=5(x+1)^2,$$
so the equation becomes $5(A+I)^2=0$, i.e., $(A+I)^2=0$.

Taking $A=-I$ (the scalar matrix $-I$): $(A+I)^2=0^2=0$. ✓

More generally, any $A=-I+N$ where $N$ is a real nilpotent matrix with $N^2=0$ works; such $N$ exists in every size $\geq 1$ (e.g., $N=0$, or a direct sum of $2\times 2$ nilpotent Jordan blocks and zero blocks).

**Conclusion.**

The set of real numbers $\alpha$ for which such a matrix exists is $\{2\}$.

